WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
The loop engineering checklist starts with choosing the right pattern.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.040
First decide whether the workflow needs an explicit loop at all.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.940
If ordinary multi-item processing already expresses the work, keep the workflow direct.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.240
If a node's native batching or pagination fits the API cleanly, use that feature.

5
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:30.020
Reach for Loop Over Items or a condition-controlled return connection only when the workflow needs controlled release, a repeated multi-node body, custom pacing, or state-based continuation.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.980
The next stage is structure.

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.440
Define exactly what one iteration means.

8
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:41.380
It may be one customer, one batch of twenty-five records, or one API page.

9
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:48.520
Identify where the loop body begins, where it ends, and what operation must complete before the return connection fires.

10
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.100
Place one-time work on the done path.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:58.560
Avoid designs where completion notices, summaries, or final writes sit inside the repeated body by accident.

12
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.280
Pacing comes next.

13
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.660
Choose a batch size that matches the risk of the downstream operation and the limits of the external service.

14
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.760
Add deliberate waits when the next batch should not start immediately.

15
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.660
Keep retry delay separate from normal pacing delay.

16
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.560
A retry handles a specific failed attempt.

17
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.380
Pacing controls the rhythm of successful work.

18
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.080
Both settings should be chosen from evidence rather than guesswork.

19
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.440
Protection is about bounded behavior and preserved meaning.

20
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:34.060
Every condition-controlled loop needs a valid exit rule, a positive continuation rule, and a practical guard against infinite repetition.

21
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.080
Preserve stable identifiers through transformations so the workflow can prove which record produced which result.

22
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:49.140
Treat non-idempotent writes carefully because a retry can duplicate a side effect if the destination cannot recognize the repeated operation.

23
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.740
The checklist should be used before production data and again after the first real run.

24
00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:01.120
Before production, it exposes missing guards, misplaced completion actions, and unclear ownership of item identity.

25
00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:07.620
After the run, it helps compare the intended iteration with the actual evidence in the execution data.

26
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:13.000
That habit turns loop design from a visual wiring exercise into an operational review.

27
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.320
It is useful to write the checklist result beside the workflow in plain operational language.

28
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:30.900
State which pattern was chosen, what one iteration means, what stops the loop, what protects side effects, and how success is counted for the next operator during routine maintenance.

29
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.880
That short record becomes a future maintainer's map when the workflow needs tuning.

30
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.160
Verification closes the design.

31
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:45.860
Operators need to see expected items, processed items, skipped items, failed items, retries, current state, and exit reason.

32
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.880
They need evidence that all intended business operations happened, not merely a green execution status.

33
00:02:51.980 --> 00:03:01.820
A reliable n8n loop has an explicit purpose, visible progress, preserved data identity, bounded retry behavior, and a provable exit.
